restrictions:
  - "Vulnerabilities: heat and fire do double damage to a Water/Ice Yaganar, cold and ice double to a Fire Yaganar; all are vulnerable to magic and most M.D. weapons."
  - "Its growing elemental look marks it as inhuman, so blending in or disguise becomes impossible, and some foes treat it as a monster or an Elemental. It misreads human customs, laws and morals."
  - "Psionics: none."
  - "Magic: only the Elemental (Warlock) spells of its own element that the levels grant; no other spell casting."
  - "Cybernetics and bionics: not possible."
  - "Equipment beyond the starting list: a hat, a satchel, a comb or brush, several empty containers or sacks, odds and ends, and a waterskin or container holding 1D4 gallons rather than one. Rarely wears body armor, but carries one weapon per W.P. with 1D4+1 reloads; other gear as its job or military assignment requires. Often hauls its team''s gear."
  - "Money: 1D4x1,000 credits, plus 1D4x1,000 more in tradable goods. Most care little for money and spend it on friends and people in need."
extraction_notes: "Rifts World Book 36: Sovietski printed 73-78 (cache p073-p078, cache page = printed folio + 0 for printed 38-177), a text layer; the stat block on printed 75 and the experience table (printed 224, cache p220, column Yaganar (Elemental Being)) read off 110 dpi renders and agree with the cache. Heading Yaganar R.C.C. on printed 73; tag line Yaganar - Optional Player Character or NPC on printed 74; the block ends on printed 78 above Other D-Bees in Russia. || ELEMENT: the book splits M.D.C., P.P.E., Spd, skills and every level bonus by element. Each element is a variant (air, earth, fire, water) carrying its attribute_dice Spd, mdc_base, ppe_base, bonuses and skills_additional; a parallel pick-one Element ability carries that element''s spells and the per-level text, because a variant cannot carry magic. || M.D.C.: P.E. number plus the element amount per level starting at level one; mdc_base gains the amount on each level-up and the level-one amount is a pools.mdc bonus on the variant (Earth adds its level-one +10). Later M.D.C. and P.P.E. jumps (levels 3, 6, 7, 10, 11, 12) are progression text marked add by hand, since a pool bonus at a level is not applied. || BONUSES: unconditional level bonuses are bonuses.at_level on each variant; Earth''s +1 save vs magic is stored as spell_magic. Conditional ones (+1 dodge in the air, +2 Perception involving its element) are progression text. || SPELLS: Elemental/Warlock spells of its own element, as the catalog held them on 2026-11-22 (Air:, Earth:, Fire:, Water: rows). 1D4+4 at level 7 and 1D4+2 at level 11 are stored as the most the roll can give (8 and 6), with the roll in the pick''s note. || SKILLS: Land Navigation printed (20%) is read as +20% beside the other bonuses. The three languages are spoken at a fixed 92%. Pilot Boats (ALL) is every Boat: row the catalog held that day, Submersibles included. Related skills: two at each of levels 2, 5 and 9 from Cowboy, Domestic, Weapon Proficiencies and Wilderness; the book prints a total of two at levels 2, 5 and 9. No Hand to Hand upgrade price is printed, so no hand_to_hand block. No secondary skills. || LIFE SPAN: the race page gives 1D6x10+12 years near-human plus 1D4x10+6 elemental; the stat block gives 1D4x10+20 years or level 15. Both are on the yaganar creatures row; neither is stored here. || NOT STORED: Experience Level 4-8 for NPCs; nicknames, size, weight, habitat, slave value 2D6x10,000, allies and enemies are in GM Notes and on the yaganar creatures row."
---

## Lore

The Yaganar are among the oddest D-Bees in Russia. From a distance one
passes for a person; up close it looks like a frozen corpse that got up and
walked: matted white hair, cracked white or pale blue skin, sunken cheeks,
long arms and ribs that show. Its eyes are one solid color and seem to boil
with energy, and few can hold its gaze. Russians named it for the witch
Baba-Yaga, and many think it an Ice Elemental, a demon or something worse.

Every Yaganar tells the same story: it remembers nothing before the moment
someone first spoke to it, naked and dirty at the edge of a field or a road.
Folk tales say each is made of one element, and guess which from its
coloring: pale or blue for water and ice, white or grey for air and storm,
yellow or brown for earth, red or bronze for fire. Other tales call them
imprisoned Elementals, a sorcerer''s failed army, or revived Gypsy witches.
Few Russians consider that they might simply be D-Bees with elemental power.

Young Yaganar are eager, curious and loyal, delighting in pouring water,
playing with flame or running in the rain, and they take quickly to the
humans who treat them kindly. The Sovietski collects them, sets them to work
on collective farms and in factories, and once they are indoctrinated and can
call on their element, may send them to the army or police, often in
support, special operations and assault roles. Officers who distrust them
treat them like attack dogs or walking hardware, and the older, more
unpredictable ones are posted far from Sovietski towns.

With each level a Yaganar looks less human and more like its element: bark,
sand or stone for earth; ice or wet sheen for water; a lean frame and
wind-tossed hair for air; jerky-dark skin and hair like flame for fire. Its
power grows with it, it becomes quieter and more inward, and in the end it
vanishes in a burst of smoke and sparks, leaving a little of its element
behind. Whether that is death or a return home, no one knows.

## GM Notes

Also called Elemental Zombies or just Yaga, and often nicknamed for the
element (Firecracker, Rocky, Snowball, Stormy). The book calls it a hard but
rewarding character: socially awkward at first, soon far stronger than most
companions, and fated to disappear.

Alignment: any; usually Principled or Scrupulous when found, and it may
drift to Selfish or Evil depending on how humanoids treat it. Horror Factor 8
at first, 11 at level 12 and 13 at level 13.

Height 6 feet to 7 feet 4 inches (1.8-2.4 m); weight 115-200 lbs
(52-90 kg) at first, tall and gaunt. Life span unknown: the race page gives
1D6x10+12 years in near-human form and 1D4x10+6 in elemental form, the stat
block 1D4x10+20 years from its first appearance or until level 15, whichever
comes first. Many in Sovietski service die in combat long before. NPCs start
at level 4-8; player characters at level one. Its experience table is slow at
first and then quick, so a high-level Yaganar is powerful but short-lived.

Habitat: Russia and Scandinavia, less often elsewhere in eastern and northern
Europe, most of all along St. Peter''s Spine and in the wilds around Moscow;
usually travelling with humans. Slave market value 2D6x10,000 credits as
labor, guard or gladiator.

Allies: humans, Cold Born, Wolverine People, Warlocks, Elemental Fusionists,
Druids, Spirits of Light and others tied to Elemental magic or nature.
Enemies: Splugorth, demons and other evil supernatural beings,
Necromancers, Shifters, Slavers and most magic users it does not know, plus
anyone who threatens a friend. Russian Fire Sorcerers hunt it as an
abomination, and true Elementals attack it first in a fight.
